# Go Coding Standards

## Code Style

- Follow `gofmt` formatting standards
- Use `golint` and `go vet` for code quality
- Prefer explicit error handling - never ignore errors
- Use meaningful variable and function names

## Error Handling

```go
// ❌ BAD - ignoring errors
result, _ := someFunction()

// ✅ GOOD - handle errors explicitly
result, err := someFunction()
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("failed to execute: %w", err)
}
```

## Package Organization

- Keep packages focused and cohesive
- Use lowercase package names
- Export only what needs to be exported (capitalize public APIs)
- Keep main package minimal - delegate to other packages

## Testing

- Write tests for all exported functions
- Use table-driven tests when appropriate
- Name test files `*_test.go`
- Use `t.Helper()` in test helpers

## Documentation

- Add package-level comments for exported packages
- Document all exported functions, types, and variables
- Use `go doc` format for documentation
